---
/**
 * About.
 *
 * Two jobs, and the second is the one people skip: say who is behind this, and
 * say why the thing works the way it does. An About page that is only a mission
 * statement is a page nobody finishes reading and no model can quote.
 *
 * RENAME: all copy. Keep the AboutPage schema and the honesty.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
import { webPageSchema } from '../lib/schema';

const description = `Who builds ${SITE.name}, and the decisions behind how it works.`;
---
<Base
  title={`About ${SITE.name}`}
  description={description}
  path="/about"
  crumbs={[{ label: 'About', href: '/about' }]}
  schemas={[
    webPageSchema({
      type: 'AboutPage',
      name: `About ${SITE.name}`,
      description,
      path: '/about',
    }),
  ]}
>
  <section class="container-page pt-12 pb-20">
    <div class="prose-page">
      <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem">About {SITE.name}</h1>
      <p>
        Open with the single most useful fact about who makes this and why it
        exists. Not a mission statement — the reason the thing was built, which
        is usually a specific problem somebody actually had.
      </p>

      <h2>Why it works the way it does</h2>
      <p>
        This is the section that earns the page. Pick the two or three design
        decisions a thoughtful customer would question, and explain them: what
        the alternative was, and why it was rejected. A page that admits a
        trade-off is more persuasive than one that claims there are none.
      </p>

      <h2>What it deliberately does not do</h2>
      <p>
        Naming the boundary is worth more than another feature. It stops the
        wrong customer buying, which is the most expensive kind of sale, and it
        makes every claim on the rest of the site more believable.
      </p>

      <h2>Who is behind it</h2>
      <p>
        A real sentence about a real person or a real company. Vagueness here is
        read as evasion, and it is the first thing a cautious buyer checks.
      </p>

      <p>
        Questions are welcome — <a href="/contact">the contact form</a> reaches
        the person who builds this.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
